-- 목적: book_text 페이지 단위 본문 적재 (asb · 이 파일: 2of11)
-- 실행자: 팀장(Supabase SQL Editor). 워커 초안. 워커 DB 직접 쓰기 금지.
-- 근거 ADR: ADR-0056 (ADR-0056 D1·D2·D4·D5) · Accepted 2026-08-10
-- source 라벨: manifest_txt_v1
-- 이 파일 담당: 48권 / 582행 (정제 후 빈 면 1행 포함 — D7)
-- 생성기: scripts/tts_pilot/gen_book_text_sql_v2.py
-- 매핑: page_index = (P번호|json page) - 1 (0-based, D2/D11). blocks = NULL(원천에 블록 정보 없음).
-- 정제: tts_targets.sanitize() 공유(D6). 표지 행 없음(D3).
-- 인용: $$ 달러 인용. 생성 시 '$$' 포함·'$' 종결 전수 가드 통과.
-- 중복: ON CONFLICT (book_id, page_index) DO NOTHING — 기존 행을 덮어쓰지 않는다.
--
-- ★ 이 파일은 ROLLBACK; 으로 끝난다. [적재검증]까지 기대값과 일치하면
--   마지막 ROLLBACK; 을 COMMIT; 으로 직접 바꿔 타이핑해 확정한다.

-- ───────── [선검증] 트랜잭션 밖 ─────────
-- (a) 대상 권의 기존 book_text 행 수 — 기대 0. 0이 아니면 중단하고 보고할 것.
SELECT count(*) AS rows_before FROM book_text bt
  JOIN books b ON b.id = bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('19100', '19194', '19260', '1938', '19414', '19421', '19467', '19470', '19474', '19752', '19761', '19762', '19766', '19767', '19830', '19854', '19885', '19898', '19933', '19986', '20147', '20148', '20150', '20158', '20205', '20299', '20302', '20367', '20550', '20587', '20814', '2084', '20853', '20870', '20873', '20874', '20876', '20879', '20884', '20886', '20892', '20910', '20911', '20917', '20936', '2102', '2104', '2124');
-- (b) 대상 source_id 중 books에 존재하는 권 수 — 기대 48
SELECT count(*) AS books_found FROM books
  WHERE source_platform='african_storybook' AND source_id IN ('19100', '19194', '19260', '1938', '19414', '19421', '19467', '19470', '19474', '19752', '19761', '19762', '19766', '19767', '19830', '19854', '19885', '19898', '19933', '19986', '20147', '20148', '20150', '20158', '20205', '20299', '20302', '20367', '20550', '20587', '20814', '2084', '20853', '20870', '20873', '20874', '20876', '20879', '20884', '20886', '20892', '20910', '20911', '20917', '20936', '2102', '2104', '2124');

-- ───────── [적재] ─────────
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO book_text (book_id, page_index, text, blocks, source)
SELECT b.id, v.page_index, v.text, NULL::jsonb, $$manifest_txt_v1$$
  FROM (VALUES
    ('19100', 0, $$My father was brilliant and caring man. During festive season he would tell us history of Bantwane culture, and where they originate.$$),
    ('19100', 1, $$My father was working at Kroondal Platinum Mine in Rustenburg, He used to come home once a year in festive season. On the second day of the arrival he would slaughter the goat with my uncle Mokgoshi and make a big feast.$$),
    ('19100', 2, $$He used to put me on his lap when he tell us stories. One day he told us to study more than one degree because the economy of this country is volatile, one will seek employment and forced to compromise his/her qualification.$$),
    ('19100', 3, $$I remember my father. Indeed life is short.$$),
    ('19100', 4, $$My father was trapped in a mine for two weeks, without food and water.$$),
    ('19100', 5, $$The rescue team did what they can however it was not enough or should i say "too little too late. "$$),
    ('19100', 6, $$Respect life and practice safety at all times.$$),
    ('19100', 7, $$My father left not only his family behind but the the legacy.$$),
    ('19100', 8, $$Value what you have and use it efficiency, My father provided everything that i desire. His warmth hug, i still remember his smell - the strawberry fragrance. He was taller than anyone i know at Ntwane village.$$),
    ('19100', 9, $$The last time i saw my father he said that "keep your head up, the world can be mean to you. "$$),
    ('19194', 0, $$Long time ago, Hyena and Hare were great friends. They were both poor but Hare owned a donkey. He also had a very small basin, which he used to feed the donkey.$$),
    ('19194', 1, $$One hot afternoon, Hare decided to give water to his donkey. However, Hare could not fetch water from the well with his small basin. So he decided to borrow a bigger basin from Hyena. Hyena gave Hare his basin because of their friendship.$$),
    ('19194', 2, $$After getting water and giving it to the donkey, Hare went home with Hyena's basin. Two weeks later, Hyena wanted to wash clothes but Hare still had his basin. Hyena left the clothes and went to Hare's house for his basin.$$),
    ('19194', 3, $$Hare gave Hyena his large basin. He also gave Hyena a small basin. Hare said, "Your basin gave birth to a young one and since we are friends, I am giving you the two of them. " Hyena was happy and accepted the two basins.$$),
    ('19194', 4, $$A few days later, Hare wanted to water his donkey and again he went to Hyena for help. Hyena thought, "Since I will get an extra basin, I will give him my basin straight away! " Hare took Hyena's big basin and went to water his donkey. Afterwards he took Hyena's basin home with him.$$),
    ('19194', 5, $$Hyena waited to collect his basin from Hare. He wanted to give it time to give birth to a young one! When Hyena went to Hare's home to collect his basin, Hare said, "Hyena, my friend, I was just about to come to deliver the bad news. Your basin died two days ago. You know that all things that live, also die! "$$),
    ('19194', 6, $$Hyena could not believe this news. He said, "I must call bigger animals to judge the case. " So Hyena called the bigger animals for a meeting. Elephants, buffalos, hippos and giraffes arrived at Hare's home to judge the case.$$),
    ('19194', 7, $$Hare was asked to explain what happened. He said, "The first time I borrowed Hyena's basin, it gave birth to a young one and I gave him the two. Unfortunately, the second time I borrowed Hyena's basin, it died before giving birth. Whatever has life also dies. " All the animals agreed with Hare's explanation. Hyena lost his basin to Hare.$$),
    ('19260', 0, $$Once upon a time Eagle and Hen were best friends. The two of them were together and chatted from morning till dusk. Eagle's chickens couldn't wait to visit Hen's chickens and play with them.$$),
    ('19260', 1, $$One day Hen approached Eagle. "My friend," she said. "I have to ask you a big favour. Rooster's pyjama pants are torn. He is really grumpy about it. They are his favourite pants. I want to mend the pants, but I don't have a needle. May I borrow your needle? " Eagle nodded. "Of course, dear friend," she said. "But please take good care of it. It is an heirloom needle. My grandmother gave it to me. " "Of course, my friend," Hen said. She took the shining needle. "I'll return it tomorrow. "$$),
    ('19260', 2, $$Hen went away to mend her husband's pants. The next morning, as usual, Hen was on her way to Eagle. But oh, goodness – the needle was gone! Hen anxiously called her little chicks together. "Has anyone seen Eagle's needle? " she asked. "No, Mommy," the chickens cheeped. "Help me look for it," Hen said.$$),
    ('19260', 3, $$Hen and her chickens looked everywhere. In every bush. Behind every lump of grass. Inside all their nests. But Eagle's needle was gone.$$),
    ('19260', 4, $$Eagle waited the whole day for Hen to come and visit. By dusk she was getting annoyed. She flew to the farm yard. "Good evening, friend," she greeted Hen. "Why didn't you visit me today? " "I I was very busy," Hen said.$$),
    ('19260', 5, $$"And where is the needle you borrowed from me? " Eagle asked. "Hen swallowed. "Oh, Eagle I am so sorry. I lost your needle. " "Lost it? " Eagle was angry. "Don't worry," Hen said quickly. "My chickens and I will find it. It must be here somewhere.$$),
    ('19260', 6, $$Eagle was very angry. "I can't believe you lost my needle," she said. "The needle I got from my grandma. You'd better look for it. If you haven't found it by tomorrow, I will come and grab one of your chickens. " The following morning Rooster had barely started crowing when Hen called on her chicks. "Kids, you must help me look for it," she said. "We'll scratch around this whole farm yard. Eagle's needle must be here. "$$),
    ('19260', 7, $$Look carefully when you see Hen and her chickens again. Up until this day they are still scratching around in the dust, looking for Eagle's needle. And when Hen notices an eagle flying in the air, she anxiously clucks: "Quick, little chicks. Hide beneath my wings. If Eagle sees you, she will catch you. She is still annoyed about her grandmother's needle. "$$),
    ('1938', 0, $$Long long ago people didn't know anything. They didn't know how to plant crops, or how to weave cloth, or how to make iron tools. The god Nyame up in the sky had all the wisdom of the world. He kept it safe in a clay pot.$$),
    ('1938', 1, $$One day, Nyame decided that he would give the pot of wisdom to Anansi. Every time Anansi looked in the clay pot, he learned something new. It was so exciting!$$),
    ('1938', 2, $$Greedy Anansi thought, "I'll keep the pot safe at the top of a tall tree. Then I can have it all to myself! " He spun a long thread, wound it round the clay pot, and tied it to his stomach. He began to climb the tree. But it was hard climbing the tree with the pot bumping him in the knees all the time.$$),
    ('1938', 3, $$All the time Anansi's young son had been standing at the bottom of the tree watching. He said, "Wouldn't it be easier to climb if you tied the pot to your back instead? " Anansi tried tying the clay pot full of wisdom to his back, and it really was a lot easier.$$),
    ('1938', 4, $$In no time he reached the top of the tree. But then he stopped and thought, "I'm supposed to be the one with all the wisdom, and here my son was cleverer than me! " Anansi was so angry about this that he threw the clay pot down out of the tree.$$),
    ('1938', 5, $$It smashed into pieces on the ground. The wisdom was free for everyone to share. And that is how people learned to farm, to weave cloth, to make iron tools, and all the other things that people know how to do.$$),
    ('19414', 0, $$Once upon a time, there was a man who had three sons. The youngest son was the cleverest and his father liked him the most. Then the man realised that his two other sons were jealous. So when the father died, he left all his cattle to his two elder sons. He only left one ox for the youngest son. But the brothers were still jealous of him.$$),
    ('19414', 1, $$One day they said, "We've got to slaughter your ox. " He was the youngest so he couldn't protest. He said, "If that must be so, there's nothing I can do. But please give me the hide. " When they slaughtered the ox, he took the hide. After drying it, he climbed a tree with it.$$),
    ('19414', 2, $$As it got darker, a group of merchants came to sleep under the tree. In the middle of the night, the young man started beating the hide with a stick. When the stick hit the hide it sounded like thunder. The merchants thought that a storm was coming. They ran off leaving their goods behind.$$),
    ('19414', 3, $$The young man picked up the goods and took them home to his brothers. They asked, "Where did you get all these things? " He said, "The price of hides is so expensive that I got all this for my one hide. "$$),
    ('19414', 4, $$The brothers quickly slaughtered their cattle. They went to the market and called, "Hides for sale! Hides for sale! " The people at the market said, "We don't want your hides! " They were very angry and said to their brother, "You fooled us, so as a punishment, we're going to burn your hut. "$$),
    ('19414', 5, $$The smart brother said, "There's nothing I can do, but please give me the ashes. " They burnt his hut. He took the ashes in a bag and went away to a rich man's hut. The rich man gave him permission to sleep in his hut. When he was going to eat, he said to the rich man, "Look after this bag, it has got a lot of valuables. "$$),
    ('19414', 6, $$In the morning he started shouting. The rich man went to ask him what was wrong. "Someone has taken all my valuables and replaced them with ashes! " moaned the young man. The rich man didn't want his reputation to be ruined. So he said, "I'll fill it up with anything you want. " He filled it with wheat, teff and all sorts of seeds.$$),
    ('19414', 7, $$The brother went home and said, "Look, ashes have become very expensive. See what I managed to buy from selling those ashes. " The brothers went and burnt their huts as well. They took the ashes to the market and shouted, "Ashes for sale! Ashes for sale! " Everybody said, "How stupid you are, who'll buy ashes? "$$),
    ('19414', 8, $$The brothers were very angry and decided to kill their younger brother. They tied him up and put him in a basket so that they could throw him over a cliff. While they were carrying the basket to the cliff, they met an old man. He said to them, "My cattle have run away, will you bring them back for me? " The brothers put down the basket and ran off.$$),
    ('19414', 9, $$The young man called from inside the basket to the old man. He asked, "What's wrong with you? " The young man replied, "They want me to become a king but I don't want to be one. " The old man said, "If I get into the basket will I become king? " And the young man answered, "Yes. "$$),
    ('19414', 10, $$The old man let the young man out of the basket and he got inside. After the brothers returned with the cattle, they picked up the basket and threw it over the cliff. The young brother took the old man's cattle back to his hut. His brothers were amazed that he was still alive, but all they said was, "Where did you get these cattle? "$$),
    ('19414', 11, $$He said, "There are many cattle at the bottom of the cliffs. If you jump off the cliffs in baskets, you will find some too. " The older brothers got into baskets. The clever brother threw the baskets over the cliffs. He lived happily ever after.$$),
    ('19421', 0, $$Mama K has been harvesting cabbages all week. Doobie, Maya and Duksie help Mama K in her vegetable garden on Saturday mornings. Today the children are going to help with counting and packing the cabbages. Then Baba K will take boxes of cabbages to sell at the market.$$),
    ('19421', 1, $$The taxi drops off the children at the garden gate. They can see the huge heap of cabbages piled up near Baba K's old truck. "Yo! What a lot of cabbages! " says Maya pointing. "There must be a thousand cabbages there! " laughs Duksie. "No way! Two hundred at the most! " disagrees Doobie.$$),
    ('19421', 2, $$Mama K is waiting at the gate. "Hello everyone, I'm happy to see you! " she greets the children. "Now let's get to work. You'll need to count the cabbages and put them into boxes, 12 cabbages in each box. I have 20 boxes. Two of you can each pack 7 boxes and one of you can pack 6 boxes. "$$),
    ('19421', 3, $$The children stand around the pile of cabbages. They talk about different ways to count the cabbages. "I think I will count mine in 2s," Maya says. "I like counting in 4s," says Duksie, "it's quicker. " "I'm going to count in 3s, just to be different! " pipes up Doobie.$$),
    ('19421', 4, $$Soon the children have filled 20 boxes. "Good work! " exclaims Mama K. "Look, there are some cabbages left over. My pigs love cabbages so let's share these with them," she suggests. "How many cabbages did you pack into boxes? How many cabbages were left over? " she asks the children.$$),
    ('19421', 5, $$The next job is to write the price on each box and then help Baba K load the boxes on his truck. They must put half the boxes on one side of the truck and half on the other side, to balance the load.$$),
    ('19421', 6, $$"We're almost done! " says Baba K. "Let's see, how many more boxes we must still put on each side? "$$),
    ('19421', 7, $$Soon all 20 boxes are loaded onto the truck. Baba K warms up the engine and drives off to market. "If I can sell all the boxes," he thinks to himself, "I'll have enough money to fix the pig pen and get a surprise for the children! "$$),
    ('19421', 8, $$Back at the farm, Mama K and the children water plants and rake leaves. By twelve o'clock they are all tired. Mama K tells them, "It's time for your treat. Can you guess what I have for you today? "$$),
    ('19421', 9, $$She goes inside to fetch their surprise. The children wait patiently for her to return while they guess what she might bring them. When she comes down the path her apron pockets are bulging!$$),
    ('19421', 10, $$"Let's see if you guessed right! What's in store for you today? " Mama K tips out her apron pockets and a pile of apples falls onto the table. "See, I was right! " Duksie squeals. Mama K tells the children, "Now remember the rule. Share fair! No one to get more or less than the other! "$$),
    ('19421', 11, $$First the children count the apples in 2s. They count: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, and 1 apple over. Equals 17 apples altogether.$$),
    ('19421', 12, $$Next, they share out the apples equally. There are 2 apples left over. The children then put their apples in their bags to take home.$$),
    ('19421', 13, $$"Let's cut the 2 apples to share between us," suggests Duksie. "How many parts of the apple will we each get? " asks Maya. "I know the answer! " smiles Doobie.$$),
    ('19421', 14, $$Just then Baba K arrives home from market. The truck is empty and Baba K is smiling. "I sold all the cabbages. Now I can fix the pig pen and buy you that soccer ball you wanted. "Yay! " shout the children. "$$),
    ('19467', 0, $$The Izinzawu family was a happy family. The two girls learned cooking and gardening from their mother. The two boys went out with their father to hunt.$$),
    ('19467', 1, $$At dinner time every day, they talked about what they had done that day.$$),
    ('19467', 2, $$The father usually warned the children not to go deep into the forest. He told the family about a strange creature that lived in the forest. "This creature is like two of us put together! "$$),
    ('19467', 3, $$One day, the girls were playing at the river. Suddenly, from behind a tree, there was a strange creature! The creature had two eyes, two ears, two arms, and two legs.$$),
    ('19467', 4, $$Shaking with fear, they ran to tell their brothers about what they had seen. But the boys didn't believe them, "The girls are being silly! " they said.$$),
    ('19467', 5, $$Eventually, curiosity got the better of the boys. They followed the girls to the river to see this strange creature for themselves.$$),
    ('19467', 6, $$They searched and searched but all they could see was the tall grass and trees.$$),
    ('19467', 7, $$Up on a tree swung the strangest creature they had ever seen. It had two arms, two hands, two legs and two feet. Instead of only one each, like them.$$),
    ('19467', 8, $$The frightened children ran home to call their parents. Soon the whole family was at the river to see this creature.$$),
    ('19467', 9, $$"Don't hurt me! " the creature said, as it hid behind the tree. Now the family could only see the creature's one foot, one leg, one hand, one arm and one eye.$$),
    ('19467', 10, $$"I am just like you," continued the creature. "The only difference is that I was made with two of almost everything. "$$),
    ('19467', 11, $$So this was how the Izinzawu family discovered that it was not a creature to be scared of. It was simply a different form of them.$$),
    ('19470', 0, $$After a long day at the farm, it was time for all the animals to go to sleep. But that night, not everyone was willing to listen to the farmer.$$),
    ('19470', 1, $$"Time for bed Miss Piggy," said the farmer. "In the pen you go. " "Oh no I won't, I won't go into the pen," said Miss Piggy.$$),
    ('19470', 2, $$"Trusty Whip, please whip Miss Piggy into the pen," said the farmer to her trusty whip. "Oh no I won't, I won't whip Miss Piggy," said Trusty Whip.$$),
    ('19470', 3, $$"Fiery Fire, please burn Trusty Whip, he won't whip Miss Piggy into the pen," said the farmer to the fire. "Oh no, I won't, I won't burn Trusty Whip," said Fiery Fire.$$),
    ('19470', 4, $$"Clear Water, please put out Fiery Fire, he won't burn the Trusty Whip, and Miss Piggy won't go into the pen," said the farmer to the water. "Oh no I won't, I won't put out the fire," said Clear Water.$$),
    ('19470', 5, $$Then along came Milky Moo, the cow. "Milky Moo, please drink Clear Water, who won't put out Fiery Fire. Fiery Fire won't burn Trusty Whip, and Miss Piggy won't go into the pen," said the farmer.$$),
    ('19470', 6, $$"MOOO! " said thirsty Milky Moo with excitement. "Of course I will drink the water. "$$),
    ('19470', 7, $$"No! No! No! Milky Moo, please don't drink me," cried Clear Water. "I will put out Fiery Fire. "$$),
    ('19470', 8, $$"Oh no you won't put me out! " shouted Fiery Fire. "I will burn Trusty Whip. "$$),
    ('19470', 9, $$"Please don't burn me Fiery Fire," cried Trusty Whip. "I will whip Miss Piggy into the pen. "$$),
    ('19470', 10, $$Miss Piggy was annoyed. "There will be no need for you to whip me, Trusty Whip," she said. "I will go into the pen. "$$),
    ('19470', 11, $$In no time at all, Miss Piggy went into her pen and was ready for bed.$$),
    ('19474', 0, $$Many people in this village were living in poverty.$$),
    ('19474', 1, $$Chief Kyemba, their leader, advised them, "Look for the wealth in the soil. " But they did not understand and did not pay attention to his words.$$),
    ('19474', 2, $$A small boy called Mutaka lived in this village. Mutaka caught a lot of birds. He used to eat the whole bird. He even ate the liver and intestines.$$),
    ('19474', 3, $$One day Mutaka caught a dove in a tree. When the dove saw that Mutaka had him, it cried out, "My friend, leave me and I tell you what you need to know. "$$),
    ('19474', 4, $$Mutaka said, "Tell me. Then I will eat you! "$$),
    ('19474', 5, $$The dove spoke while changing its colour from grey to purple. "Let me go to that tree over there, and then I will tell you more. "$$),
    ('19474', 6, $$Mutaka let the dove go and it flew to the tree. When it reached there, it laughed at Mutaka. "Wealth is in meee! " called the dove.$$),
    ('19474', 7, $$Mutaka asked, "What are you saying? " "Inside me is gold! " replied the bird. "You were stupid to release me. You have missed a lot of wealth," the dove tormented Mutaka.$$),
    ('19474', 8, $$Then the bird passed out droppings that looked like gold. It flew to a taller tree. Mutaka started to scratch the ground around the tree.$$),
    ('19474', 9, $$He called out to the villagers, "Come with me, we can all scratch for wealth in the ground. "$$),
    ('19474', 10, $$To this day, Mutaka and the villagers are scratching the ground looking for gold.$$),
    ('19474', 11, $$The dove continues to sing in the tree that, "Wealth is in meee! "$$),
    ('19752', 0, $$This is Natabo. She has six brothers.$$),
    ('19752', 1, $$Her parents give her a small pot. She loves it.$$),
    ('19752', 2, $$One day her brothers break the pot.$$),
    ('19752', 3, $$Natabo cannot find her pot. She cries and cries.$$),
    ('19752', 4, $$Natabo runs away. She climbs a tall tree.$$),
    ('19752', 5, $$Her parents find her. "Please come down," they ask.$$),
    ('19752', 6, $$Her brothers sing, "Please come down. "$$),
    ('19752', 7, $$Natabo makes fun of their song.$$),
    ('19752', 8, $$Then Natabo's friend comes. "Please come down," she sings.$$),
    ('19752', 9, $$Natabo's friend sings until she comes down from the tree.$$),
    ('19752', 10, $$The friends walk home together.$$),
    ('19752', 11, $$Everyone celebrates that Natabo is home. Natabo gets a new pot!$$),
    ('19761', 0, $$These people are disagreeing with each other.$$),
    ('19761', 1, $$"Teachers are most important. You need us to learn," says teacher.$$),
    ('19761', 2, $$"Builders are most important. You need houses," says builder.$$),
    ('19761', 3, $$"Carpenters are most important. You need furniture," says carpenter.$$),
    ('19761', 4, $$"Doctors are most important. You need us to get well. "$$),
    ('19761', 5, $$"Farmers are most important. You need us for food. "$$),
    ('19761', 6, $$"Students are most important! " says the student.$$),
    ('19761', 7, $$"Because you were all students first! " Now everyone agrees.$$),
    ('19762', 0, $$Frog and Snake are friends.$$),
    ('19762', 1, $$Frog says, "Come with me to visit my mother. "$$),
    ('19762', 2, $$"Thank you," Snake says. "Who will watch our homes? "$$),
    ('19762', 3, $$"I will watch your homes," says Monkey.$$),
    ('19762', 4, $$Frog puts white ants in a bag for her mother.$$),
    ('19762', 5, $$Frog and Snake leave at dawn. They go into a forest.$$),
    ('19762', 6, $$They meet a gang of squirrels. Snake is afraid.$$),
    ('19762', 7, $$Frog protects her friend.$$),
    ('19762', 8, $$They are hungry when they get to Mother's house.$$),
    ('19762', 9, $$They wash and get ready to eat.$$),
    ('19762', 10, $$"Snake, please sit up," says Frog.$$),
    ('19762', 11, $$Snake tries to sit up, but she is not a frog.$$),
    ('19762', 12, $$"I cannot sit up like a frog," says Snake.$$),
    ('19762', 13, $$Frog will not listen.$$),
    ('19762', 14, $$"You do not understand me. I am going home. "$$),
    ('19762', 15, $$Snake is annoyed. "I thought Frog was my friend. "$$),
    ('19766', 0, $$Mod the toad was tired of living in water.$$),
    ('19766', 1, $$"Be careful," said the turtle. "Your skin will dry out. "$$),
    ('19766', 2, $$But Mod was curious. She hopped away.$$),
    ('19766', 3, $$She looked all around.$$),
    ('19766', 4, $$She met a purple chameleon next to a puddle of water.$$),
    ('19766', 5, $$The chameleon started turning pink! Mod jumped away.$$),
    ('19766', 6, $$Then she saw a hyena starting a fire.$$),
    ('19766', 7, $$The fire spread around Mod. She was afraid.$$),
    ('19766', 8, $$The fire burned her skin. She jumped back, into the puddle.$$),
    ('19766', 9, $$But her skin was already full of blisters.$$),
    ('19766', 10, $$Mod found a home under a rock. Her new skin was dry and rough.$$),
    ('19766', 11, $$Now only frogs have smooth skin and live near water.$$),
    ('19767', 0, $$The rains did not come. The land was very dry.$$),
    ('19767', 1, $$This monkey left home to look for water and food.$$),
    ('19767', 2, $$She walked over hills and valleys.$$),
    ('19767', 3, $$She came to a place named Tirkol.$$),
    ('19767', 4, $$Monkey was very happy in this place.$$),
    ('19767', 5, $$She ate fruit and got fat. But she missed her friends.$$),
    ('19767', 6, $$So Monkey made the long journey home.$$),
    ('19767', 7, $$The other monkeys were joyful to see her.$$),
    ('19767', 8, $$"Where is this wonderful place with fruit? " they asked her.$$),
    ('19767', 9, $$"I will take you there," said Monkey.$$),
    ('19767', 10, $$The other monkeys loved Tirkol. They decided to stay there forever.$$),
    ('19767', 11, $$But the monkeys from Tirkol were worried.$$),
    ('19767', 12, $$"These new monkeys will eat all our fruit," they said.$$),
    ('19767', 13, $$The Tirkol monkeys went to attack the new monkeys.$$),
    ('19767', 14, $$"Why fight? " asked an elder. "There is enough fruit for all! "$$),
    ('19767', 15, $$This was true. So the monkeys decided to live together peacefully.$$),
    ('19830', 0, $$My name is Kafui and I have a little sister called Elinam.$$),
    ('19830', 1, $$Elinam is always happy, whenever it is time for school.$$),
    ('19830', 2, $$Their teacher Madam Esi, tells and reads interesting stories to them everyday.$$),
    ('19830', 3, $$She also gives them, storybooks to read at home.$$),
    ('19830', 4, $$My little sister is always happy to go to school.$$),
    ('19854', 0, $$There was a shepherd who had no grass to feed his pig, sheep and goat and no grains for his hen. He had no barn to keep them warm.$$),
    ('19854', 1, $$He decided to take them where they could get food and shelter.$$),
    ('19854', 2, $$The shepherd said, "I could sell them for a quarter each. " But the man was negotiating for a lower price.$$),
    ('19854', 3, $$The man said, "I only want the goat. " But the shepherd said, "These animals are friends. I want all of them to be together. "$$),
    ('19854', 4, $$The shepherd sold the sheep, the goat and the pig for a quarter each. He gave the hen for free. He was sad as he headed home.$$),
    ('19854', 5, $$He realised they weren't just animals. They were also his friends. So he went back to return the money and take his friends home.$$),
    ('19854', 6, $$"They are mine now," the man said. "I won't give them back. " But the shepherd kept begging.$$),
    ('19854', 7, $$"I realised they are not just animals but also my friends," said the shepherd. The man asked, "Why did you sell them in the first place? "$$),
    ('19854', 8, $$A neighbour heard the argument and said, "Make a barn, provide grass and grains. Then you can get your animals back. "$$),
    ('19854', 9, $$The shepherd loved his animals. He gave back the money and took home his friends to a perfect barn and plenty of grass and grains.$$),
    ('19885', 0, $$Wamaitha and her grandchildren sat outside her house. The children were excited because they really enjoyed listening to their grandmother's stories. Wamaitha began, "Long, long ago. " She paused and looked at her grandchildren's faces glow with curiosity.$$),
    ('19885', 1, $$Wamaitha continued to tell this story: Beyond the hills and valleys, was a place called Mongu. For years, people from neighbouring Kitwe occupied Mongu. The people in Mongu were forced to work for long hours with little pay. All that they produced was taken to Kitwe. The little that remained was sold back to Mongu people at high prices.$$),
    ('19885', 2, $$One day, the people of Mongu held a meeting. They had decided that enough was enough. They wanted their land back from Kitwe. They discussed the odds of winning. They knew that Kitwe had a strong army and better weapons. There were many ideas, but even after a long time they had no solution. Just as they were about to give up, a young man stood up and said, "I think I know a way. "$$),
    ('19885', 3, $$They were all curious and asked, "Tell us! " The young man began, "My grandfather told us about a man named Ubuntu who lives across the forest. He grows a fruit called uhuru which gives people the strength to do anything. Let me find this man. " They were all silent and did not know what to do. Since no one else had a better idea, they said, "You go and find this Ubuntu. "$$),
    ('19885', 4, $$The young man set out to find Ubuntu. After five days of going through the forest he came out to find the house. He was very happy. He found Ubuntu, who asked, "What do you want? " The young man answered, "I want uhuru. " As he said that, the other man's eyes grew bigger with surprise. It was so long since anyone had gone in search of the uhuru fruit.$$),
    ('19885', 5, $$They talked for a long time. Ubuntu decided to give the young man the fruit. He said, "Listen carefully. Once you eat this fruit you will acquire wisdom which will help your people to win independence. You will be the leader of Mongu. Rule with humility. " He gave the young man a small round fruit and added, "Follow my instructions and your land will prosper. Now, go, and tell no one about me. ''. "$$),
    ('19885', 6, $$The young man went back to Mongu. Just as Ubuntu had said, they were able to chase away the people of Kitwe and get back their land. A celebration was held. The young man was praised for his courage. He became the first leader of independent Mongu. This man was wise and governed his people well. He served them with humility and considered them in every action he took.$$),
    ('19885', 7, $$Wamaitha said, "My dear grandchildren, the fruit of freedom comes to each one of us at a given point. Be wise enough to use it well. Remember your actions do not only affect you but everyone around you. " The content grandchildren went into the house. Wamaitha sat back and watched them go. She hoped the stories she told them would build them into better people and leaders for tomorrow.$$),
    ('19898', 0, $$It was one of those happy summer days meant only for play. Mammas and pappas were standing, sitting and strolling about. Children small and tall were playing in the sands, beaches, grass and water. Some were cycling. Some played ball. Some just mucked about. Everyone was jolly.$$),
    ('19898', 1, $$The sun beamed down from high in the sky on top of the world. He glowed with pleasure to see everyone having such a good time. Nothing pleases him more than the chorus of children's happy laughter. But clocks all over the land were ticking towards the day's end. "I can't go to bed now," the sun thought, "it will spoil everyone's fun. " The sun did not want to be a spoilsport. "I am staying put," he said gleefully.$$),
    ('19898', 2, $$It was fast approaching the time when mamas and papas call children for clean ups. It was time for children to wave goodbye to friends. It was getting to the dreaded bedtime. The sun was not moving. He stubbornly remained rooted at the same spot. Normally the shadows would grow longer, the later it got. But on that day, the shadows did not grow any longer or shorter or wider. The sun refused to go to bed.$$),
    ('19898', 3, $$Below, children were still playing, laughing, skipping and jumping, although it was long past their playtime. Parents, being parents, looked with worry at their watches. They were puzzled. What should they do? The clocks showed it was past playtime, but the sun still stood at playtime!$$),
    ('19898', 4, $$In faraway villages, parents were saying, "Children it is time for bed. " And children, being children, responded, "But the sun is still shining brightly. " Mammas and pappas looked at each other. Then they looked up to the sky and wondered, "Should we send our children to bed? It is late, but the sun is still staying put at the midday spot. "$$),
    ('19898', 5, $$It was getting so late now that the moon and the stars were ready to appear. The moon whispered sternly to the sun, "You must go to bed immediately. It is my time to be awake. " The moon warned the sun, "If you do not go to bed now, the children will never go to bed, neither will the animals nor the plants. The world will turn upside down. "$$),
    ('19898', 6, $$But the sun shook his head. "Look," he said, "the children love it. "They want me to stay. They want to have more fun. " The moon was starting to lose patience. She urged the sun more sternly. "Go to the bed! " said the moon. "No! " said the sun. A furious wind joined in. He shrieked in an angry voice, "To bed, to bed, to bed. " But the sun stood firm. "$$),
    ('19898', 7, $$What could the moon do? The world was turning upside down. Animals that were supposed to sleep during the day and come out at night, kept on sleeping. Flowers that were supposed to close their buds when the sun sets, stayed open. Little bugs that were supposed to come out at night stayed in their little homely holes.$$),
    ('19898', 8, $$The moon asked the wind, "Please help me to change the sun's mind. " So the wind tried a different approach. He said softly to the sun, "Please go to bed now, little sun. You must be very tired after so much shining? "$$),
    ('19898', 9, $$Then the wind gushed strongly, "To bed, now, sun. " But this did not work either. The wind howled stronger and louder, causing a wave of air to sweep the earth. Mammas, pappas, children, other legged and winged creatures grabbed their things and scrambled for cover. Worried parents called for their children to hurry inside. It was getting later, later and later. Children were getting tired. Some were even dozing off.$$),
    ('19898', 10, $$"What about whistling a lullaby to the sun? " twinkled one little star to the wailing wind. "I guess I could try," huffed the wind. So he began to whistle a soothing slow melody. It was the same bedtime lullaby his mother whistled to him when he was a little breeze. The sun's eyelids began to get heavier and heavier.$$),
    ('19898', 11, $$The sun couldn't stay awake any longer. He finally collapsed into bed. The moon and the stars returned. The animals which stayed awake during the day could also now fall asleep. The flowers could close their buds and finally sleep. The night animals could come out.$$),
    ('19898', 12, $$Because the sun had gone to bed so late, the next morning, he was so tired that he could not wake up. The moon whispered to the sun, "Wake up now, lazy bones. " However, the sun did not hear her. The wind whistled noisily but the sun did not wake up. Although it was supposed to be morning, it remained night. The world was upside down.$$),
    ('19898', 13, $$On earth the animals remained sleeping. The flower buds that were supposed to open, remained closed. Children remained in bed. Because they went to bed so late, they were too tired to wake up on time for breakfast, or to go to school. Those children who did manage to open their sleepy eyes, closed them again, thinking it was still night because the sun was not up. They returned to Slumberland again. The louder the wind howled to wake the sun, the tighter some children pulled their blankets over their heads.$$),
    ('19898', 14, $$Because the sun did not wake up, the flowers did not spread open their petals. Bees and butterflies stayed in their beds because the flowers were still asleep. The birds did not sing to welcome the morning. And although it was late morning, it was as dark as the middle of the night.$$),
    ('19898', 15, $$The moon and stars shouted at the sun to wake up. They were tired now and wanted to go to bed, after being up all night. The wind wailed to wake up the sun. The birds chirped loudly. Dogs barked. Cats meowed. Cocks crowed. All the noise made the sun wake up with a jolt.$$),
    ('19898', 16, $$Because the sun did not wake up in time, because he went to bed so late, the world was upside down. Everything and everybody was late. Parents woke up late. Children woke up late. Parents went to work late. Children went to school late.$$),
    ('19898', 17, $$The birds' singing was late. The insects' buzzing was late. The flowers were late in opening their buds. Everybody, including the sun, was grumpy, sleepy and confused. Everybody was angry with the sun.$$),
    ('19898', 18, $$The sun was embarrassed. He promised he would never again go to bed so late. "I will always try to go to sleep on time, and wake up on time," said the sheepish sun to the upside down world.$$),
    ('19933', 0, $$I see fire again.$$),
    ('19933', 1, $$The fire is burning brightly.$$),
    ('19933', 2, $$The fire can cook meat and vegetables.$$),
    ('19933', 3, $$The fire can keep me warm.$$),
    ('19933', 4, $$The orange light of the fire gives me light when it is dark.$$),
    ('19933', 5, $$The fire crackles and pops.$$),
    ('19933', 6, $$Fire is powerful and can be dangerous.$$),
    ('19933', 7, $$We must be careful when lighting fires or playing with fire.$$),
    ('19986', 0, $$In one village, animals ate all the food. The people were unhappy.$$),
    ('19986', 1, $$One family moved to the hills to find enough food.$$),
    ('19986', 2, $$Other families joined them when they saw them looking better.$$),
    ('19986', 3, $$The children learned different ways of survival. They climbed trees like monkeys did.$$),
    ('19986', 4, $$They also spoke less and less. The noses and the teeth of the parents became longer.$$),
    ('19986', 5, $$With time, they forgot how to talk. Their noses became wider. They laughed at each other.$$),
    ('19986', 6, $$They had become baboons. They could not live together any more. They went in different directions.$$),
    ('20147', 0, $$"I have a very big problem," cried Chief Short and Round.$$),
    ('20147', 1, $$"I have lost my spring horn," said Chief Short and Round.$$),
    ('20147', 2, $$"I will find your spring horn," said Little Jojo. "I will look at Tata Rectangle's house. "$$),
    ('20147', 3, $$Little Jojo and Tata Rectangle looked everywhere but they could not find the spring horn.$$),
    ('20147', 4, $$"Go and see if Mama Triangle has the spring horn," said Tata Rectangle.$$),
    ('20147', 5, $$Little Jojo stopped at his house to pour a glass of cold water.$$),
    ('20147', 6, $$Jojo visited Dudu Diamond and asked if she would go with him.$$),
    ('20147', 7, $$"It’s not far but I will pack some food to take with," said Dudu.$$),
    ('20147', 8, $$Dudu and Jojo stopped to eat and rest at the bottom of the triangle mountain.$$),
    ('20147', 9, $$When they were half way up the mountain, they saw a strange spider web.$$),
    ('20147', 10, $$Jojo and Dudu arrived at Mama Triangle’s house.$$),
    ('20147', 11, $$"The chief did not leave his spring horn here," said Mama Triangle.$$),
    ('20147', 12, $$Mama Triangle knew where they could find the spring horn.$$),
    ('20147', 13, $$It was evening when they arrived at Chief Short and Round’s house.$$),
    ('20147', 14, $$Mama Triangle pointed at the chief. "You are wearing your spring horn on your head. "$$),
    ('20147', 15, $$Chief Short and Round gave Little Jojo the spring horn to blow. They were happy.$$),
