    ('20299', 0, $$Meet Ndalo. He is 10 years old and in Grade 5 at the village school. Ndalo always comes top of his class. "What's his secret? " his schoolmates ask themselves.$$),
    ('20299', 1, $$Ndalo sometimes earns R80 a week in pocket money. He spends most of the pocket money on books at the second-hand book shop in his village.$$),
    ('20299', 2, $$Meet Pendo, the family's milking cow. She produces over 20 litres of milk per day. Ndalo's father sells the milk.$$),
    ('20299', 3, $$First, Ndalo gives Pendo a bunch of carrots. He gives her exactly eight carrots a day. Each day he picks the carrots from his father's vegetable patch, enough for a day at a time.$$),
    ('20299', 4, $$After that, Ndalo takes Pendo to graze in the fields. His father warned him to watch the time and not give Pendo more than three quarters of an hour to graze.$$),
    ('20299', 5, $$Next Ndalo gives Pendo water. Without water Pendo won't produce milk, no matter how much Ndalo feeds her. Ndalo fetches buckets of water from the village pump because there isn't one nearby.$$),
    ('20299', 6, $$Then, Ndalo takes Pendo to his father to get her daily 12 kg bag of grain. Ndalo calculates that's almost 90 kg a week.$$),
    ('20299', 7, $$Ndalo's father milks Pendo twice a day. She produces about 24 litres of milk a day, so about 12 litres for each milking.$$),
    ('20299', 8, $$His father pours the milk from the big bucket into smaller two litre cans or one litre bottles. He then sells the milk for R8 a litre.$$),
    ('20299', 9, $$For each litre of milk sold, his father gives Ndalo 50c. That doesn't sound like a lot, but if his father sells 24 litres a day, it adds up to quite a bit. Can you tell how much?$$),
    ('20299', 10, $$Ndalo also loves to drink Pendo's milk. His father says that milk helps to build strong bones and teeth and makes a person fit and healthy.$$),
    ('20299', 11, $$Ndalo never forgets to thank Pendo at the end of each day. He rewards her with extra carrots or spinach leaves that he finds lying around the garden.$$),
    ('20302', 0, $$Gobani was a very tall man. The villagers called Gobani the Gentle Giant, because he was quiet and friendly. He was not like the bad giants you read about in some stories.$$),
    ('20302', 1, $$Thimba lived next door to Gobani. It made him sad to see Gobani struggling with his short hoe, or bending down to get through his door.$$),
    ('20302', 2, $$Thimba asked Gobani, "How tall are you? " Gobani estimated, "I am about 2,5m tall. " He lay down on the bed and explained, "I know my mattress is 1,5m long. You can see that my legs hang over the end for at least another metre. So that means I must be at least 2,5m tall. "$$),
    ('20302', 3, $$Thimba could now start working on his secret plan.$$),
    ('20302', 4, $$At the village square, Gobani got off his bicycle, and a swarm of friendly villagers surrounded him. The village chief welcomed him. "Hello Gentle Giant," he said. "Young Thimba told us that you face problems in your life, being so tall. "$$),
    ('20302', 5, $$The villagers worked around the clock to make sure everything Gobani needed was made the correct size.$$),
    ('20302', 6, $$Next the villagers helped Gobani build a taller door frame with a new door for his house. It was a bit taller than Gobani, so he no longer had to stoop down to get inside. Just right!$$),
    ('20302', 7, $$Next came a brand new bed and mattress with extra-long sheets and blankets to cover Gobani's long body from head to toe.$$),
    ('20302', 8, $$The chief asked one of the village steel workers to make a giant-sized bicycle for Gobani. It had a basket that was big enough for both Thimba and Choke to sit in.$$),
    ('20302', 9, $$The carpenters made Gobani a new table to sit at with legs that are at least 1,5m tall. Now when he sits down to eat, his feet touch the ground.$$),
    ('20302', 10, $$Gobani's new hoe made it easy for him to plant and harvest. His garden became a forest! Gobani leaves baskets of fruit outside his front door for the villagers, to show how thankful he is for their help.$$),
    ('20367', 0, $$It is the first day of the term$$),
    ('20367', 1, $$Welcome back to school pupils, beamed Mrs Clark$$),
